/* book exam traps: Using 250 N/mm² or the yield stress of mild steel as if it applied to prestressing steel. High-tensile steel has no yield point; the code works with fpu and a proof stress. | Reading M40 as a tensile strength. It is the characteristic cube compressive strength at 28 days; the flexural tensile strength of M40 is only about 4.4 N/mm². | Writing that strong concrete "resists" shrinkage. Higher grades simply creep and shrink less, so the losses are smaller. | Confusing relaxation with creep: relaxation is stress loss in the steel at constant strain; creep is strain growth in the concrete at constant stress. | Mixing units: mm² × N/mm² gives N. Divide by 1000 only at the end to get kN. */
